This fruit basket program is written in IronRuby, which Microsoft has 
released, along with IronPython, as dynamic languages for the .NET 
Framework.  IronRuby is available at
http://IronRuby.net

The program uses the Layout by Code support of the Homer.NET library -- 
distributed as part of the Homer application framework, HomerApp, at
http://EmpowermentZone.com/appsetup.exe

or .zip for a manual install.

IronRuby works with most libraries of the standard Ruby distribution, 
available at
http://ruby-lang.org

including Ruby on Rails, the popular web application framework, available at
http://RubyOnRails.org

This compatibility with standard libraries is an advantage over 
IronPython, which does not work with most standard Python libraries.  A 
disadvantage of IronRuby is that it is not currently possible to compile 
code to an executable that can be distributed independently of the 
interpreter, something IronPython can do.  Applications written with 
either dynamic language require distribution of .dll files that support 
the Dynamic Language Runtime (DLR) of the .NET Framework.  Homer.NET 
support also involves .dll files that need to be present in the 
application directory.  Both dynamic languages include a read - evaluate 
- print loop (REPL) for testing code in an interactive, console mode 
environment.

   Source code of lbc_fruit.rb is pasted below.

Jamal

